{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1550597-comic-book-stories-that-you-dislike","id":1550597,"title":"Comic book stories that you dislike?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-05-02T10:57:42-07:00","id":1550597,"title":"Comic book stories that you dislike?","content":"My mini debate with Heavy Arms inspired me to post a new topic about what comic book story arcs from DC, Marvel, Image or others that you disliked strongly? Without any further ado.<br /> <br /> From DC Comics back in the 1990's years of bad writing turned Hal Jordan into a bland character and they gave new writer Ron Marz who started in issue 46 only four issues to get rid of Green Lantern Corps, turn Hal Jordan into a villian and introduce a new Green Lantern by issue 50. Even excellent writers like Peter David, Mark Waid, Kurt Busiek, Paul Dini would be having a hard time getting a good plot ready and Marz isn't on their level.<br /> <br /> It made Green Lantern fans angry and they went after both DC and Ron Marz. I can understand the dislike for the editorial board that made the decision, but they did get quite personal on there attacks on Ron Marz and act shocked when his interaction with the Hal Jordan camp was lukewarm. <br /> <br /> Marvel Comics in the 1990s decided to bring back the Spider Man clone story and fans were disgusted that the Spider Man that they had been following for years was a clone and the original was Ben Reliy and what was meant to be a short story arc was extended and we had filler stories that served no real purpose. The storyline did huge damage to the brand and the attempt to fix it was worse.<br /> <br /> Iron Man The Crossing in which Tony Stark is turned into a villian by the schemes of Kang/Immortus and betrayed his teammates and dies to be replaced by a 18 year old version of himself.<br /> <br /> These are the ones that stand out to me, so what are your story arcs that you disliked?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-05T22:29:17-07:00","id":1550723,"title":"","content":"I'm wordy as hell so I apologize beforehand. tl;dr Red Robin and &quot;To Infinity,&quot; the Avengers story where Hood jobs with the Infinity Gems. There's a lot of low hanging fruit like Chuck Austen's work or Amazons Attack (where Themyscira, the tiny Amazon island, decides to invade the United States, and some surprising superheroes decide to join them), but I figure that's not worth hashing out there's tons of &quot;Worst Stories in Comics&quot; listicles for those.<br /> <br /> Red Robin. I'm a Batman fan. Was never much of a Robin one. But I gave Dick Grayson a chance by reading his Nightwing line and it was really good, sold me on the character. So I read the Robin line, this one featuring Tim Drake, and it was okay, didn't really sell me on him being a necessary part of Gotham but he was a decent hero who stood on his own. A reboot later and they reintroduced him in Red Robin and man I didn't like it. There's a lot of terms that can be thrown around for the type of writing but I think the most modern term would be &quot;glazing.&quot; There's 26 issues (that's just over two years) and across it four different women fall for him, two of them after trying to kill him and one of them when still doing so. He pretty causally outsmarts every opponent he faces, and they all &quot;respect him,&quot; even Ra's al Ghul who turns over control of his League of Assassins to Tim. I always thought one of the drawbacks of the character was that he basically start off at &quot;peak&quot; of human ability. Despite being just some rich kid who lived next door to Bruce Wayne and hadn't seemed to ever set foot out of Gotham, he was able to slip into being Robin without needing much if any training. But then they started a weird trend of everyone in the Bat-family saying he was the best of them, he'd be a better detective than them, a better fighter than them. And this run was the biggest part of it. Like it would flat out have not surprised me if in this run Joker popped up, hugged him and told Tim he was a better hero than Batman before turning himself in.<br /> <br /> I like villains. And the Hood, Parker Robbins, was one of my favorites in Marvel. He was a draw for me, so I normally don't read Marvel comics unless he was popping up. But man his writing really took a bad turn. He started like his name suggested, he's a &quot;Hood,&quot; just a sort of low criminal, not associated with superstuff. But it becomes a more fitting name when he finds a red hood and magic boots that grant him some weak superpowers (the hood lets him turn invisible as long as he holds his breath, the boots let him levitate, basically stepping upward, so he's still at regular human moving speed). Since his origin he's really yo-yo'd with power, becoming an organized crime mastermind to rival the Kingpin one story, then basically a nobody. He almost became the Sorcerer Supreme, granted power from the Norn stones, learned magic and marksmanship and gathers huge amounts of money for that kind of thing, he did become the Ghost Rider, wiping out all the crime families of Chicago. Then nothing. Anyways at one point he finds out about the Infinity Gems, each hidden away from the world by one of the Illuminati (Iron Man, Dr. Strange, Professor X, Reed Richards, Black Bolt, and I think Namor). The Avengers catches wind about halfway through him collecting them all. Uatu the Watcher is watching Parker because this is the first time a human would have ever held all the Gems. There's a big fight and despite tanking hits from Red Hulk, Thor, and Namor he just kind of drops two of the gems and then the last one just poofs to Iron Man, who has the others, because (it's explained) if you have some of them you don't even need to bother looking for the others. &quot;They want to be as one,&quot; and they don't &quot;like&quot; the Hood. Never a fan of the Infinity Gems I thought this was a particularly bad showing for them. The gems are rewarded for their favor by being split apart and locked away again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-07T06:40:02-07:00","id":1550789,"title":"","content":"That sounds like a bad Marvel Comics story and I guess that they forgot that they can be used in unison after The Living Tribunal ruling way back in the day.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts